{{define "content"}}
<vox-hero eyebrow="AI agents" heading="Let AI agents help, with a person approving every change" data-i18n-attr="heading,eyebrow">
  <p data-i18n>
    Connect an AI agent to the console over MCP. It can read your fleet
    freely. Whenever it wants to change something, it asks &mdash; and
    nothing runs until someone approves.
  </p>
</vox-hero>

<section class="site-section">
  <h2 class="vox-ts-xxl" data-i18n>Read freely, ask before acting</h2>
  <div class="vox-prose">
    <p data-i18n>
      The agent can look at nodes, facts and reports, node groups, the tasks
      and plans your code offers, and the results of jobs. What it can see
      is exactly what its token allows: the permissions, the node groups
      and the tasks you chose when you issued it.
    </p>
    <p data-i18n>
      To run a task, run Puppet, run a plan or patch nodes, the agent
      submits a request. The console checks it exactly as it would check
      yours, stores it, and tells the agent it is waiting. Nothing has
      started.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

<vox-step-indicator>
  <vox-step>The agent asks to run something</vox-step>
  <vox-step>The console checks it and holds it for approval</vox-step>
  <vox-step>A person reviews exactly what would run, and where</vox-step>
  <vox-step>Approved, it runs as the agent; rejected or ignored, it never does</vox-step>
</vox-step-indicator>

{{screenshot "approvals"}}

<section class="site-section">
  <h2 class="vox-ts-xxl" data-i18n>What you approve is what runs</h2>
  <div class="vox-prose">
    <p data-i18n>
      A request records the task and its parameters, and the exact nodes it
      will act on, at the moment the agent asked. Sensitive values are
      sealed and never shown. The person approving sees all of it, and
      approving starts precisely that &mdash; not whatever the agent might
      ask for a moment later. A request nobody decides expires.
    </p>
    <p data-i18n>
      The job it starts shows that an AI agent asked for it, which agent,
      and who approved it.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

<vox-grid min="240px" gap="lg">
  <vox-card heading="Tokens that expire" data-i18n-attr="heading">
    <vox-icon slot="icon" name="key"></vox-icon>
    An agent token always has an expiry, is limited to the node groups and
    tasks you name, works only at the agent endpoint, and can be revoked at
    any moment.
  </vox-card>
  <vox-card heading="People decide" data-i18n-attr="heading">
    <vox-icon slot="icon" name="people"></vox-icon>
    Only someone who could run the task on those nodes themselves can
    approve it, and never the person who issued the agent's token.
  </vox-card>
  <vox-card heading="On the record" data-i18n-attr="heading">
    <vox-icon slot="icon" name="activity-log"></vox-icon>
    Every request, approval, rejection and expiry is audited under the
    agent's own name, alongside the person who decided.
  </vox-card>
</vox-grid>

{{screenshot "agent-tokens"}}

<vox-callout variant="tip" heading="Off until you turn it on" data-i18n-attr="heading">
  <p data-i18n>
    The endpoint does not exist until you enable it, and it has its own
    rate limit and size limits. Agents that do not speak MCP can use the
    console's documented REST API with an ordinary token &mdash; without the
    approval step, so give those tokens only what you would give a script.
  </p>
</vox-callout>
{{end}}
